Hi [Manager],

I pulled the Illinois EPA Source Water Assessment Program factsheet data for =
the eight water systems you listed and screened every well. The workbook is a=
ttached (Illinois_Well_Screening.xlsx).

Bottom line: 7 of the 33 wells meet all three criteria (depth 160-200 ft, San=
d & Gravel aquifer, active per well description). Farmer City is the stronges=
t candidate: it has four of the seven and the highest reported pumpage.

Recommended wells (all Status "A", Sand & Gravel, 200 ft minimum setback, Max=
 Zone 0)

1. Farmer City WL47648, Well 9 - 188 ft - pumpage 26,478,500 (highest of all =
33 wells)
2. Farmer City WL47646, Well 7 - 180 ft - pumpage 21,976,200
3. Farmer City WL47647, Well 10 - 181 ft - pumpage 0 reported (verify use)
4. Farmer City WL01130, Well 11 - 192 ft - pumpage 0 reported (verify use)
5. Weldon WL45047, Well 3 - 167 ft - pumpage 5,500,000
6. Weldon WL45048, Well 4 - 163 ft - pumpage 3,503,040
7. Waynesville WL40006, Well 6 (Village Hall) - 162 ft - pumpage 4,319,500

Ranking is my judgment: all seven pass the screen equally, so I ordered them =
by reported pumpage as a rough proxy for proven yield, with the zero-pumpage =
Farmer City wells below the pumping ones. Farmer City is also the only system=
 with several qualifying wells, which gives redundancy. Weldon and Waynesvill=
e are small systems (about 450 and 510 people served) and look like second-ti=
er options.

Why the others dropped out
- Springerton, Enfield, Crossville, Bartlett: no well passes. Springerton, En=
field and Crossville wells are all abandoned and 60-410 ft deep. Bartlett wel=
ls are sealed, emergency or disconnected, or are the wrong depth or aquifer (=
Bartlett Well 6 is Sand & Gravel but is an emergency well at 67 ft).
- Norris City: both wells are active Sand & Gravel but only 97 and 124 ft dee=
p.
- Near misses: Waynesville WL40005 (Well 8, the primary well) is 207 ft, 7 ft=
 over the limit. Weldon WL01791 (Well 6) is 350 ft. Weldon WL45049 is marked =
INACTIVE.
- Farmer City WL47643 and WL47645 are in range but marked ABANDONED, and WL47=
644 is 90 ft.

Caveats before we act on this
- These are municipal public-supply wells serving the towns. The factsheet da=
ta does not tell us whether any of this water is available for industrial use=
. Next step would be to approach the owner and, as I understand it, check Ill=
inois EPA and local permitting. I have not researched the permitting.
- The factsheet gives no yield or capacity, and the pumpage units are not sta=
ted. Pumpage is only a rough indicator. We should get pumping capacity, water=
 quality and any use restrictions from the owner and Illinois EPA before shor=
tlisting further.
- The screen follows the criteria on the well description text, as specified.=
 The Status column also shows "A" for all seven.
- Our required water volume was not part of this exercise, so I have not test=
ed whether any of these wells can supply it.

Workbook contents
- "All Wells": all 33 wells, with filters on every column plus Yes/No screeni=
ng columns for each criterion and a "MEETS ALL CRITERIA?" column. Passing row=
s are shaded green.
- "Potential Wells": the 7 passing wells with notes below the table.

Source: Illinois EPA Source Water Assessment Program Factsheets, https://data=
services.epa.illinois.gov/swap/factsheet.aspx (community systems: Farmer City=
 IL0390150, Springerton IL1930450, Bartlett IL0314120, Enfield IL1930200, Cro=
ssville IL1930150, Weldon IL0390350, Norris City IL1930350, Waynesville IL039=
0300). Retrieved 2026-11-06.

Happy to walk through it or to widen the search to more systems if these don'=
t work out.

